import LegalLayout from '../layouts/LegalLayout.astro';

const FAQ = [
  {
    q: 'What model does Lumecon use?',
    a: 'A regionalized input-output model built from the national economic accounts, extended with household and government accounts (a social accounting matrix formulation) so induced effects and the government tax-and-spend cycle can be estimated alongside direct and indirect effects.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Is Lumecon an alternative to IMPLAN or REMI?',
    a: 'Organizations evaluate Lumecon as an alternative to IMPLAN, RIMS II and REMI. The input-output foundation is shared across the field; Lumecon differs in its fully public, citable data foundation, the per-figure lineage with source tags, reservation and trust-land modeling and flat subscription pricing. Lumecon is not affiliated with or endorsed by IMPLAN or REMI.',
  },
  {
    q: 'How does Lumecon handle suppressed government data?',
    a: 'Federal disclosure rules withhold some county-sector figures. Where a suppressed sector clearly exists in a region, Lumecon estimates its employment from wider employment-per-establishment patterns and tags the figure as estimated, so a reviewer can always tell reported values from estimates.',
  },
  {
    q: 'How are reservations and trust lands modeled?',
    a: 'As modeled geographies constructed from the federal boundary files for American Indian and Alaska Native areas and the data located within them, with regional purchase behavior estimated for the region itself. Household income includes transfer income, which is a meaningful share of income in many reservation economies.',
  },
  {
    q: 'What do direct, indirect and induced effects mean?',
    a: 'Direct effects are the analyzed activity itself: its spending, wages and jobs. Indirect effects happen at the suppliers it buys from. Induced effects come from workers spending their earnings in the region. Lumecon reports the three layers separately and shows how each total is composed.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Are Lumecon results estimates?',
    a: 'Yes. Results are decision-support estimates and are labeled as such wherever they appear. Every analysis states its data year and assumptions, and exports carry the full tables behind each figure so the estimates can be reviewed.',
  },
];

const faqJsonLd = [
  {
    '@context': 'https://schema.org',
    '@type': 'FAQPage',
    '@id': 'https://lumecon.ai/methodology#faq',
    mainEntity: FAQ.map((item) => ({
      '@type': 'Question',
      name: item.q,
      acceptedAnswer: { '@type': 'Answer', text: item.a },
    })),
  },
];
---

<LegalLayout
  title="Methodology | Lumecon"
  description="How Lumecon builds an economic impact analysis: regionalized input-output modeling on public data, household and government accounts for induced effects, reservation and trust-land geographies, and a lineage behind every result."
  canonical="https://lumecon.ai/methodology"
  keywords="economic impact methodology, input-output modeling, direct indirect induced effects, BEA input-output accounts, social accounting matrix, location quotient, RAS balancing, economic multipliers, IMPLAN methodology comparison, REMI comparison, tribal economic impact methodology, traceable results"
  extraJsonLd={faqJsonLd}
>
  <h1>Methodology</h1>
  <p class="legal-subhead">
    How a Lumecon analysis is built, in plain language and in the equations behind it.
  </p>

  <h2>The core model</h2>
  <p>
    Lumecon analyses are built on input-output modeling, the same analytical foundation used across
    the economic impact field for decades. An input-output model maps how industries in a region buy
    from and sell to each other, so a change in one place (a new program, an expansion, a budget)
    can be followed through the purchases, wages and household spending it sets off.
  </p>
  <p>
    We keep that proven core and supplement it with higher-frequency public indicators and newer
    estimation methods, so an analysis can better reflect current economic conditions rather than
    relying exclusively on the vintage of the underlying benchmark tables.
  </p>
  <p>
    In matrix form the model is compact. Each industry&rsquo;s production recipe is summarized by a
    technical coefficient, the amount it purchases from every other industry per dollar of its own
    output:
  </p>
  <div class="eq" role="math" aria-label="a sub i j equals z sub i j divided by x sub j">
    <span aria-hidden="true"
      ><var>a</var><sub>ij</sub> = <span class="eq-frac"
        ><span><var>z</var><sub>ij</sub></span><span><var>x</var><sub>j</sub></span></span
      ></span
    >
  </div>
  <p>
    where <var>z</var><sub>ij</sub> is what industry <var>j</var> buys from industry{' '}
    <var>i</var> and <var>x</var><sub>j</sub> is industry <var>j</var>&rsquo;s total output.
    Collecting the coefficients into the matrix <var>A</var>, the total output{' '}
    <var>x</var> required to satisfy a change in final demand <var>f</var> solves the Leontief system:
  </p>
  <div
    class="eq"
    role="math"
    aria-label="x equals the inverse of the quantity I minus A, multiplied by f"
  >
    <span aria-hidden="true"
      ><var>x</var> = (<var>I</var> &minus; <var>A</var>)<sup>&minus;1</sup>{' '}
      <var>f</var></span
    >
  </div>
  <p>
    The inverse matrix (<var>I</var> &minus; <var>A</var>)<sup>&minus;1</sup> is the Leontief inverse.
    It carries every round of purchasing at once: the direct requirement, the suppliers&rsquo; requirements,
    the suppliers&rsquo; suppliers and so on. Extending the table with household and government accounts,
    described below, lets the same arithmetic carry induced effects.
  </p>

  <h2>The data behind every analysis</h2>
  <p>
    Lumecon is built from authoritative public datasets widely used in economic and regional
    analysis: U.S. Census Bureau programs (ACS, LODES, QWI, CBP, TIGER/Line), the Bureau of Economic
    Analysis Input-Output Accounts, BLS QCEW, USDA NASS, USAspending, FRED and NOAA. The difference
    is what we do with it and what we add to it.
  </p>

  <h2>From national relationships to your region</h2>
  <p>
    The industry-to-industry relationships in the national accounts describe how the U.S. economy as
    a whole buys and sells. To analyze a county, a state or a reservation, those relationships are
    adjusted to the region: local industry mix, employment and wages come from the regional data
    listed above, and regional purchase behavior estimates how much of each purchase is met by
    suppliers inside the region rather than imported from outside it. Regional self-sufficiency is
    estimated sector by sector with location quotients in the Flegg family, which compare how
    specialized the region is in each sector with the wider economy, and the adjusted table is then
    rebalanced with biproportional (RAS) scaling so the accounting identities still hold. Smaller
    and more specialized regional economies keep less of each dollar, and the model reflects that.
  </p>
  <p>
    The starting point is the simple location quotient, which compares a sector&rsquo;s share of
    regional employment with its share nationally:
  </p>
  <div
    class="eq"
    role="math"
    aria-label="S L Q sub i equals e sub i superscript r over e superscript r, all divided by e sub i superscript n over e superscript n"
  >
    <span aria-hidden="true"
      >SLQ<sub>i</sub> = <span class="eq-frac"
        ><span><var>e</var><sub>i</sub><sup>r</sup> / <var>e</var><sup>r</sup></span><span
          ><var>e</var><sub>i</sub><sup>n</sup> / <var>e</var><sup>n</sup></span
        ></span
      ></span
    >
  </div>
  <p>
    where <var>e</var> counts employment, <var>i</var> indexes the sector, and{' '}
    <var>r</var> and <var>n</var> mark the region and the nation. The Flegg-family refinements Lumecon
    uses compare selling and purchasing sectors pair by pair and discount local purchasing by regional
    size:
  </p>
  <div
    class="eq"
    role="math"
    aria-label="F L Q sub i j equals C I L Q sub i j times lambda star, where C I L Q sub i j equals S L Q sub i over S L Q sub j, and lambda star equals the logarithm base 2 of 1 plus e superscript r over e superscript n, all raised to the power delta"
  >
    <span class="eq-line" aria-hidden="true"
      >FLQ<sub>ij</sub> = CILQ<sub>ij</sub> &times; &lambda;*, where CILQ<sub>ij</sub> ={' '}
      <span class="eq-frac"><span>SLQ<sub>i</sub></span><span>SLQ<sub>j</sub></span></span></span
    >
    <span class="eq-line" aria-hidden="true"
      >&lambda;* = [log<sub>2</sub>(1 + <var>e</var><sup>r</sup> / <var>e</var><sup>n</sup>)]<sup
        >&delta;</sup
      ></span
    >
  </div>
  <p>
    The exponent &delta; (delta) sets how strongly a smaller regional economy discounts local
    purchasing. It is calibrated against observed regional data rather than fixed by assumption, and
    every regional coefficient is capped so no local share exceeds one. After regionalization, the
    biproportional (RAS) step rescales the table iteratively:
  </p>
  <div
    class="eq"
    role="math"
    aria-label="A superscript k plus 1 equals R superscript k, times A superscript k, times S superscript k"
  >
    <span aria-hidden="true"
      ><var>A</var><sup>(k+1)</sup> = <var>R</var><sup>(k)</sup>{' '}
      <var>A</var><sup>(k)</sup>{' '}
      <var>S</var><sup>(k)</sup></span
    >
  </div>
  <p>
    where <var>R</var> and <var>S</var> are diagonal matrices of row and column scaling factors, updated
    each round until the table converges on the regional control totals. The result is a regional table
    whose accounting identities hold just as they do in the national one.
  </p>

  <h2>Direct, indirect and induced effects</h2>
  <p>
    Every result separates the three layers of activity. Direct effects are the project's own
    spending, wages and jobs. Indirect effects happen at the suppliers the project buys from.
    Induced effects come from workers spending their earnings locally. The layers are always shown
    separately, so a reviewer can see how the total is composed rather than taking one number on
    faith.
  </p>
  <p>The multipliers used across the field summarize the same decomposition:</p>
  <div
    class="eq"
    role="math"
    aria-label="The Type 1 multiplier equals direct plus indirect effects, divided by direct effects. The Type 2 multiplier equals direct plus indirect plus induced effects, divided by direct effects."
  >
    <span class="eq-line" aria-hidden="true"
      >Type I = <span class="eq-frac"><span>direct + indirect</span><span>direct</span></span></span
    >
    <span class="eq-line" aria-hidden="true"
      >Type II ={' '}
      <span class="eq-frac"><span>direct + indirect + induced</span><span>direct</span></span></span
    >
  </div>
  <p>
    Lumecon reports the layers themselves rather than leading with a single multiplier, and
    validation checks flag any multiplier outside the ranges credible for an economy of the
    region&rsquo;s size.
  </p>

  <h2>The household and government accounts</h2>
  <p>
    Induced effects come from a social accounting matrix formulation. The input-output table is
    extended with a household account that receives labor income and spends it back into the region,
    and a government account that collects taxes and returns them as procurement and transfers.
    Household income includes transfer income, which matters in regions where transfers are a large
    share of income, including many reservation economies. Local spending propensities govern how
    much of each household dollar recirculates in the region rather than leaking out, so induced
    effects stay tied to the region&rsquo;s real spending patterns.
  </p>

  <h2>Suppressed and small-area data</h2>
  <p>
    Federal disclosure rules withhold some county-sector figures so individual employers cannot be
    identified. Where a suppressed sector clearly exists in the region, its employment is estimated
    from wider employment-per-establishment patterns, and wages fall back through a stated hierarchy
    of sources. Every figure carries a source tag (reported, apportioned or estimated) so a reviewer
    can see exactly which numbers came straight from the data and which were estimated.
  </p>

  <h2>Geographies that follow the real economy</h2>
  <p>
    Analyses run at the county, state and national level, and reservations and trust lands are
    treated as first-class regions rather than approximations. Where an analysis covers both a state
    and homelands, the two scopes are reported side by side with the subset relationship stated
    explicitly, so nothing is double-counted.
  </p>

  <h2>Reservations and trust lands as modeled geographies</h2>
  <p>
    Reservation and trust-land regions are constructed from the federal boundary files for American
    Indian and Alaska Native areas and from the employment, industry and demographic data located
    within those boundaries. They are modeled geographies with their own regional characteristics,
    and results for a reservation are estimated for that region itself. In this branded context we
    use homelands as the plain-language umbrella term for reservation and trust-land geographies.
    Tribal government analysis is an active area of methodological work for us, and this page will
    grow as that work does.
  </p>

  <h2>What the headline measures mean</h2>
  <p>
    Jobs supported counts the jobs supported by the analyzed activity over the analysis period; it
    is a job count, and where a conversion basis matters the analysis states it. Labor income is
    wages, salaries and benefits. GDP contribution is the analysis&rsquo;s value added, the measure
    that corresponds to a region&rsquo;s contribution to GDP. Economic output is the total value of
    production the activity supports, which includes value added plus intermediate purchases. Tax
    impacts are estimates of the revenue the activity supports, reported by level of government:
    federal, state and local, and tribal government where the analysis includes one.
  </p>

  <h2>Data vintages</h2>
  <p>
    Every analysis states the data year it runs against, and that year is shown with the results
    (for example, 2025 data). Historical analyses run from 2015 to present where the underlying data
    support it. Where source datasets carry different vintages, the analysis uses the most recent
    vintage available for each source at the stated data year.
  </p>

  <h2>Higher-frequency adjustments</h2>
  <p>
    The benchmark input-output tables update on the schedule of the agencies that publish them.
    Between benchmarks, higher-frequency public series for employment, wages and prices update the
    levels the model scales against, where the data support it. The structural relationships between
    industries remain tied to the benchmark tables, so the proven core stays intact while the levels
    stay current.
  </p>

  <h2>Every number has a lineage</h2>
  <p>
    Each headline figure carries a trace: which effect layer it comes from, which operations
    generate it and which industries it lands in, each with its share. Exports include the full set
    of tables and the assumptions behind them, so the person defending the number in a council
    meeting, a boardroom or a grant review holds the same detail the model used.
  </p>

  <h2>Validation and human review</h2>
  <p>
    The software checks that tables cross-foot, that scopes nest correctly and that inputs fall in
    plausible ranges. Estimated inputs and model outputs are checked against plausibility bounds,
    including multiplier ranges credible for a small regional economy, and a run that fails a
    critical check stops rather than shipping a doubtful number. Cedar surfaces the assumptions it
    makes when structuring inputs and flags gaps it cannot resolve. The person running the analysis
    reviews and confirms those assumptions before results are finalized: automation carries the
    mechanical work, and people remain responsible for the analytical decisions.
  </p>

  <h2>How Lumecon compares</h2>
  <p>
    Lumecon shares its analytical foundation with the established tools in the field. IMPLAN and
    RIMS II are built on the same input-output framework and the same federal statistical system;
    REMI adds dynamic forecasting models on top of that framework. Lumecon&rsquo;s differences are
    in construction and delivery: a fully public, citable data foundation; household and government
    accounts for induced and tax-and-spend effects; a per-figure lineage with source tags;
    reservations and trust lands as modeled geographies; and one flat subscription covering every
    supported geography. Lumecon is not affiliated with or endorsed by IMPLAN, REMI or any other
    platform named here.
  </p>

  <h2>What these figures are</h2>
  <p>
    Lumecon results are decision-support estimates. They describe likely economic relationships
    given the data and assumptions stated in each analysis; they are estimates, and they are labeled
    as such everywhere they appear. See the <a href="/glossary">glossary</a> for the terms used across
    analyses, and write to <a href="mailto:contact@lumecon.ai">contact@lumecon.ai</a> with methodology
    questions.
  </p>

  <h2>Common questions</h2>
  {
    FAQ.map((item) => (
      <>
        <h3 class="legal-subhead">{item.q}</h3>
        <p>{item.a}</p>
      </>
    ))
  }
</LegalLayout>
